🚀 APK Build Internal Process
    1 Source Code (.kt / .java / .xml)
            ↓
    2 Compilation (javac / kotlinc)
            ↓
    3 Bytecode Conversion (DEX)
            ↓
    4 Resource Processing (AAPT2)
            ↓
    5 Manifest + Resource Merge
            ↓
    6 Packaging (APK)
            ↓
      7 Signing
            ↓
    8 Alignment (zipalign)
            ↓
    9Final APK Output

🧩 Step-by-Step Internal Explanation
Step 1: Source Compilation
    Your Kotlin (.kt) or Java (.java) files are compiled into .class files.
    Tools used:
        kotlinc → for Kotlin
        javac → for Java
    Output: .class files inside build/intermediates/.

Step 2: Bytecode to DEX (Dalvik Executable)
    All .class files are converted into DEX (Dalvik Executable) format.
    Tool: D8 (replaced the older dx).
    Output: classes.dex
        This is the code that the Android Runtime (ART) executes on devices.

Step 3: Resource Compilation
    XML resources (layouts, strings, colors, drawables) are compiled.
    Tool: AAPT2 (Android Asset Packaging Tool v2).
    Output:
        resources.arsc → compiled resource table.
        Binary XML files.

Step 4: Manifest & Resource Merging
    AndroidManifest.xml from:
        Main source
        Libraries
        Dependencies
    All merged into a single manifest file.
    Gradle plugins handle this merging phase automatically.

Step 5: Packaging
    Tool: AAPT2 or Gradle packager
    It packages:
        classes.dex
        resources.arsc
        res/
        AndroidManifest.xml
        lib/ (native libraries)
    Produces an unsigned APK.

Step 6: Code Shrinking & Obfuscation (Optional)
    Tool: R8 (combines ProGuard + D8).
    It:
        Removes unused classes/methods.
        Renames symbols for obfuscation.
        Reduces APK size.
        Output: optimized DEX + mapping file.

Step 7: APK Signing
    Android requires every APK to be digitally signed.
    Tools:
        apksigner
        Gradle automatically signs using your keystore (.jks or .keystore).
    Types:
        Debug signing → Android Studio auto-generates.
        Release signing → Developer signs with private key.

Step 8: Zipalign
    Tool: zipalign
    Optimizes APK alignment for faster loading on devices.
    Ensures data sections are aligned to 4-byte boundaries.
    Produces final optimized APK.

Step 9: Final Output
    The build system outputs:
    app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
    or
    app/build/outputs/apk/release/app-release.apk


🚀 APK Components Inside
    When built, an APK contains:
        AndroidManifest.xml → app metadata
        classes.dex → compiled Kotlin/Java bytecode
        resources.arsc → compiled resources (layouts, strings)
        res/ → app resources
        lib/ → native libraries (.so files)

🚀 Which File Is Called First When App Launches?

When you tap an app icon, Android follows a specific launch sequence:
Step-by-step launch flow:
1 AndroidManifest.xml
    The system reads this file first.
    It defines:Application components (Activities, Services, BroadcastReceivers)
    App permissions

    The launcher activity (the first screen).
    Example:

    <activity android:name=".MainActivity">
        <intent-filter>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent-filter>
    </activity>
    Here, MainActivity is the entry point — it’s the first file executed when the app starts.


2 MainActivity.kt / MainActivity.java
    onCreate() method runs first in your app’s code.
    Example:
    class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {
        override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
            super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
            setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)
        }
    }


3 System Flow: Launcher -> AndroidManifest.xml -> MainActivity -> onCreate()

🧠 In Short:
Step	Description
1️⃣	    Tap app icon
2️⃣	    Android reads AndroidManifest.xml
3️⃣	    Finds <intent-filter> with MAIN + LAUNCHER
4️⃣	    Launches that Activity (e.g., MainActivity)
5️⃣	    Calls onCreate() method → loads UI